WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.830
You type one letter to
a friend: a capital A.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:04.830
Send.

3
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.450
Zoom into your phone's
memory: no letters anywhere.

4
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.250
Just 0s and 1s.

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.970
Your friend's phone is made
by a different company.

6
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.310
Yet the same A pops up.

7
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.150
So how does a computer store a letter,
when all it can store is numbers?

8
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.710
In this mission, you'll see that
a computer stores everything,

9
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.330
even text, as binary numbers.

10
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.570
You'll find out why two devices must
agree on what each pattern means,

11
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.670
or text loses its meaning.

12
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.750
And you'll spot a pattern: letters
follow each other in alphabetical order.

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.790
Your mission: explain how a computer
stores the letter A when it can only store

14
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.520
numbers.
